# Chaos Solver, Chaos Cloth, and Chaos Destruction Tools

The `chaos` toolset contains 23 tools. Nine native UE 5.6 routes expose guarded
Chaos Solver and Chaos Cloth state that is incomplete or unsafe through generic
Python reflection; the existing fourteen tools retain the isolated Chaos
Destruction learning and cinematic workflows documented below.

## Chaos Solver and Chaos Cloth native surface

Read-only inspectors:

- `inspect_chaos_solver_settings(actor_name="", actor_label="",
  world_context="auto", include_runtime_state=true, max_results=16)` reports
  matching `AChaosSolverActor` configuration and, when requested, native
  runtime solver state. Runtime output includes the six allowlisted global
  CVar overrides that UE 5.6 applies after actor configuration, including each
  raw value, active state, inferred effective value, and source. An empty
  selector may list bounded matches; mutation commands require an exact,
  unique actor. `max_results` is clamped to `1..256`.
- `inspect_chaos_cloth_asset(cloth_asset_path, lod_index=-1,
  property_name_filter="", include_property_values=true,
  include_property_flags=true, include_dataflow_nodes=true,
  max_properties=512, max_nodes=256)` reports cloth collection/property
  metadata and the Dataflow nodes that own durable asset configuration.
  `max_properties` is clamped to `1..4096` and `max_nodes` to `1..2048`.
- `inspect_chaos_cloth_simulation_mesh(cloth_asset_path, lod_index=0,
  weight_map_name="", vertex_start=0, max_vertices=4096, face_start=0,
  max_faces=4096, include_positions=true, include_normals=false,
  include_indices=true, include_weight_values=true)` reads the native generated
  simulation collection that `UChaosClothAsset` does not expose to Unreal
  Python. It reports bounded pages of positions, normals, triangle indices, and
  selected weight values together with total counts, available weight-map names,
  `topology_md5`, the compiled solver map count/MD5/match, and copy-ready
  Dataflow/node identity for a guarded write. For one selected map, copy
  `sim_vertex_count` to `expected_vertex_count`, `topology_md5` to
  `expected_topology_md5`, and `selected_weight_map_md5` to
  `expected_weight_map_md5`; the equivalent hash in the full map list is
  `weight_maps[].values_md5`. Copy `dataflow_asset_path` directly, then select
  the exact `weight_map_dataflow_nodes[]` entry and use its `node_name` as the
  writer's `node_name`, `node_type` as `expected_node_type`, and
  `node_state_md5` as `expected_node_state_md5`. The inspector does not certify
  a future mutation path: run the writer once with `dry_run=true` and the same
  target/value arguments, then copy that result's `path_md5` to
  `expected_path_md5` for apply. Page through large meshes by
  advancing `vertex_start` and `face_start`; each page size is bounded by the
  native handler.
- `inspect_chaos_cloth_component(actor_name="", actor_label="",
  component_name="", model_index=0, lod_index=-1, world_context="auto",
  include_runtime_properties=true, max_properties=512, max_results=16)` reports component
  settings, model/LOD property collections, and available native simulation
  metrics without changing the component. `max_results` bounds components and
  `max_properties` is one response-wide runtime-property budget shared across
  all returned components; their clamp ranges are `1..256` and `1..4096`.

Guarded mutators:

- `configure_chaos_solver_settings(settings, actor_name="", actor_label="",
  world_context="editor", expected_current=null, dry_run=true,
  save_level=false)` changes only the native solver-setting allowlist. Supply
  at least one non-empty actor selector: `actor_name` or `actor_label`.
- `configure_chaos_cloth_component(actor_name="", property_updates=null,
  component_settings=null, component_name="", actor_label="", model_index=0,
  lod_index=-1, world_context="auto", expected_current=null,
  allow_intrinsic_recreate=false, dry_run=true,
  recreate_simulation="auto", save_level=false)` changes existing runtime
  cloth properties and reviewed component settings. Supply at least one
  non-empty actor selector: `actor_name` or `actor_label`. Intrinsic changes that
  require simulation-model reconstruction are blocked unless
  `allow_intrinsic_recreate=true`; `recreate_simulation` accepts `auto`,
  `always`, or `never`. A call accepts at most 256 property updates.
- `configure_chaos_cloth_dataflow_node(cloth_asset_path, node_name,
  property_values, expected_node_type="", expected_dataflow_asset_path="",
  expected_current=null, allow_connected_inputs=false,
  regenerate_asset=true, save_assets=false, dry_run=true)` edits a uniquely
  identified, reviewed
  Cloth Dataflow node. `property_values` maps top-level property names to
  Unreal export-text strings copied from or formatted like the inspector's
  `export_text` values, with at most 64 fields per call. Apply mode requires the
  exact non-empty
  `expected_node_type` and `expected_dataflow_asset_path` returned by
  inspection and requires `regenerate_asset=true`. Connected inputs are
  blocked by default; `allow_connected_inputs=true` is an explicit opt-in for
  the selected property and its nested Dataflow inputs. An actual Dataflow
  mutation is blocked when the graph is owned by any other loaded/unsaved Cloth
  Asset or referenced by another on-disk package; fan-out mutation is not
  admitted by this route. A canonical same-value request is returned as an
  explicit no-op without regeneration, dirtying, saving, or a shared-graph
  mutation error.
  Regeneration and saving are reported separately. If apply, regeneration,
  readback, or saving fails, the handler attempts a semantic restore of the
  selected node properties, regenerates the cloth result, verifies that restore,
  and re-saves the restored packages when an initial save was attempted.
- `configure_chaos_cloth_weight_map(cloth_asset_path, node_name,
  weight_map_name, weights=null, fill_value=null, lod_index=0,
  expected_node_type="", expected_dataflow_asset_path="",
  expected_vertex_count=-1, expected_topology_md5="",
  expected_weight_map_md5="", expected_node_state_md5="",
  expected_path_md5="", require_runtime_binding=true, save_assets=false,
  dry_run=true)` updates one
  supported `FChaosClothAssetWeightMapNode` using either an explicit normalized
  `[0,1]` value per simulation vertex or one normalized constant fill. Apply mode
  requires the exact node type, Dataflow asset path, vertex count,
  `topology_md5`, generated `selected_weight_map_md5` (or the selected
  `weight_maps[].values_md5`), and `node_state_md5` returned by fresh inspection.
  Pass those values as the corresponding `expected_*` arguments described
  above, execute a fresh dry-run with the intended values, review its exact
  active/unfrozen `before.downstream_path.hops` and top-level `path_md5`, then
  apply with that hash as
  `expected_path_md5`. Apply rejects a changed path even when the asset, node,
  topology, and map hashes are otherwise unchanged. The command regenerates the cloth asset, verifies independent
  node, generated-collection, and compiled solver-array readback, and requires
  the runtime map binding by default. Before mutation, the node's Collection
  output must be one exclusive passthrough chain to the selected asset terminal
  and requested LOD; internal Dataflow branches and alternate terminal/LOD paths
  fail closed. If the map is consumed as `MaxDistance` or
  `FixedEndWeightMap`, the source and every required downstream generator must
  also be active, unfrozen, and part of a recognized semantic regeneration
  path. Legacy `MaxDistance` admits Simulation Default Config only for the
  canonical `MaxDistance` map when the node input has no pre-existing property
  schema. Modern `MaxDistance` requires Simulation Max Distance Config followed
  by Long Range Attachment Config v2, with `FixedEndSet` connected directly to
  that selected node's `KinematicVertices3D` output and custom tether generation
  disabled;
  legacy `FixedEndWeightMap` admits only its reviewed Long Range Attachment
  generator path. Unknown, remesh, disabled/frozen, and custom derivation routes
  fail closed instead of assuming that Dataflow regenerated the solver inputs.
  Independent readback canonicalizes final collection and compiled-solver
  tethers as `(dynamic vertex, kinematic vertex, reference-length bits)` and
  requires identical vertex count, tether count, and MD5. When the reviewed
  path produces a kinematic selection, it also canonicalizes the collection's
  `KinematicVertices3D` selection and the compiled solver `VertexSets` entry as
  sorted unique vertex indices, requiring matching count and MD5. Apply succeeds
  only when every required check passes after regeneration;
  rollback repeats both checks against the restored state. Failures report
  semantic-path, tether, kinematic-selection, regeneration, readback, save, and
  rollback evidence separately.
- `create_legacy_chaos_clothing_from_skeletal_sections(
  source_skeletal_mesh_path, output_skeletal_mesh_path, section_configs,
  dry_run=true, save_assets=true, use_source_physics_asset=true,
  create_vroid_lower_body_cloth_physics_asset=false)` duplicates a Skeletal Mesh to one new
  temporary-root output, then creates and binds one engine Clothing asset for
  each requested LOD 0 section. Every `section_configs` item requires
  `section_index`, `expected_material_slot`, and `max_distance_cm`; it may also
  provide `material_path`, which is required when the source slot has no
  assigned material. The selected material must already be compiled for both
  Skeletal Mesh and Clothing usage and is assigned only to the duplicated
  output. Blender's
  `Cloth_PinGuide` vertex-color red channel becomes the legacy Chaos
  `MaxDistance` paint map (black = 0 cm/pinned, white = configured free
  distance). If the FBX importer has discarded those vertex colors, the route
  reports and uses the garment section's top 18% of local Z range as a
  deterministic pinned fallback. The route creates a light-fabric Chaos config, validates each
  material slot, pin/free guide state, MaxDistance map, shared config, and
  final source/render section binding before an optional exact-package save.
  Set `use_source_physics_asset=false` when a unit-scaled import has an
  oversized Physics Asset; this omits collision only from the generated
  Clothing assets while leaving the duplicated Skeletal Mesh Physics Asset and
  Character Capsule movement collision intact. For a VRoid `J_Bip_*` skeleton,
  `create_vroid_lower_body_cloth_physics_asset=true` takes precedence and
  generates a separate hips/upper-leg collision asset proportionally sized
  from the left/right reference upper-leg lengths, leaving both source assets
  unchanged. Failed apply attempts remove every newly owned output asset before
  returning whenever deletion succeeds. It rejects
  PIE/SIE, existing outputs, invalid sections, material mismatches, and empty
  guide states. The source mesh is not modified and finished assets contain no
  UnrealMCP type or reference.

The Dataflow writer is a constrained reflective surface, not a fixed list of
every Epic cloth-node field. It accepts only nodes classified as Chaos Cloth,
excludes the generic `FDataflowNode` base fields, and admits top-level editable
bool, numeric, enum, byte, string, name, or struct properties. Array, map,
object, transient, deprecated, and instance-disabled properties remain
blocked. Inspect first and use only properties marked
`supported_for_mcp_write=true`.

All native mutators default to `dry_run=true`, are classified as high-risk
writes, require dispatcher confirmation for apply mode, and use strict
post-mutation readback. `expected_current` provides optimistic concurrency
checks. Mutation results follow `mutation_result_v1` with `before`, `after`,
`delta`, and explicit verification state; a missing or failed postcondition
reports failure. Apply-mode no-ops keep both the top-level and nested mutation
evidence at `attempted=false`/`performed=false`. Solver/component saving starts only after native readback and
proxy checks pass and targets the exact actor/component package rather than the
owning persistent world. No-op patches do not create an undo record, notify the
solver, reconstruct the proxy, dirty a package, or save a previously dirty
package; `save_skipped_no_change=true` makes that explicit. An actual requested
save is rejected before mutation when its exact target package is already dirty,
so the tool cannot persist unrelated pre-existing edits. Dataflow apply and
rollback save each exact graph/cloth package and require the package to be clean
after `SavePackages` before reporting durable success. Save requests reject
transient objects, PIE/transient/temp/memory/compiled-in packages, and actors or
components with a transient outer; apply mode cannot claim durability for state
that UE will not serialize. Component recreation is
rejected before mutation while `p.ClothPhysics=0`; one-shot teleport actions
are delayed until readback, recreation, and saving have succeeded. Solver and
component failures attempt semantic rollback of serialized settings, targeted
runtime properties (exact raw Low/High vectors, string, and flags), teleport
state, the original present/absent proxy state, and the original package dirty
state. Runtime updates write the exact discovered collection key; they do not
invoke the engine interactor's private alias fan-out. If a failed request already
recreated an existing proxy, UE 5.6 exposes no way to restore its previous live
particle/velocity state. The response therefore distinguishes proxy presence
from full simulation-state restoration and returns `partial_target_modified=true`
with `do_not_retry=true` for that case. Successful editor-world Solver writes also register an
Undo/Redo resync so restored `AChaosSolverActor::Properties` are applied back to
the live solver when one exists; apply and rollback report their enqueue evidence
separately. The MCP callback enqueues `ApplyConfig` directly and does not emit a
second synthetic actor property-change event after Undo.

Dataflow failure responses additionally report each rollback phase. A complete
semantic rollback keeps regenerated packages dirty when they were not re-saved
and retains the editor transaction because regenerated derived data is not a
byte-exact package restore. An incomplete rollback likewise retains the undo
record and tells the caller to undo before continuing.
The evidence includes `rollback_attempted`, `rollback_regenerated`,
`rollback_properties_verified`, rollback save statuses,
`rollback_semantic_restore`, `rollback_save_verified`,
`rollback_transaction_retained`, both package dirty flags, and the aggregate
`rollback_succeeded`. Semantic in-memory restoration is reported independently
from durable rollback saving; a save failure never hides a successful
in-memory restore, and a semantically incomplete restore is never persisted.

The native boundary validates top-level scalar types and required object/array
container shapes before resolving or loading an Unreal target, then validates
every nested value before any mutation. Strings, booleans, numbers, objects,
and arrays are not cross-coerced;
integer indices must be finite exact integers inside `int32` and their
route-specific range. This remains true for raw `execute_batch` subcommands,
which do not pass through FastMCP's per-tool Pydantic schema.

The Python boundary guards the explicit `cloth_asset_path` and
`expected_dataflow_asset_path` selectors. Its recursive scope is exactly Cloth
component `property_updates`/`component_settings` and Dataflow
`property_values`. Of those nested payloads, component settings and Dataflow
property values can reach persistent level/graph state; runtime-only property
updates are conservatively screened but are not claimed as persistent. Actor
selectors and the Solver's native scalar/enum allowlist are not described as
asset-reference guards. A rejection returns
`mcp_dependency_reference_rejected` with only a bounded field path and stable
reason; it never echoes the rejected value. Finished solver, cloth, level, and
Dataflow content may depend on engine Chaos modules but must not persist
`/Script/UnrealMCP`, `/UnrealMCP`, or another MCP-only reference. Durable asset
or level writes remain opt-in through `save_assets` or `save_level`.

### Solver payload

`settings` and top-level `expected_current` use flat keys. The inspector returns
the exact flat map as each actor's `expected_current_fields` and the accepted
write keys as `writable_setting_names`.

- Iteration integers: `position_iterations`, `velocity_iterations`, and
  `projection_iterations`, each in `0..255`.
- Non-negative numbers: `collision_cull_distance`,
  `collision_max_push_out_velocity`, `cluster_connection_factor`, and every
  `*_filter_min_*` field. Initial-overlap depenetration accepts exactly `-1`
  (disabled/effectively unlimited) or a non-negative number.
- Enum string: `cluster_union_connection_type`; round-trip the inspected enum
  name.
- Destruction integers: `per_advance_breaks_allowed` and
  `per_advance_breaks_reschedule_limit` are non-negative;
  `clustering_particle_release_throttling_min_count` and `_max_count` accept
  `-1` or a non-negative integer. Min must not exceed max when both values are
  non-negative.
- Booleans: `optimize_for_runtime_memory`, `generate_collision_data`,
  `generate_break_data`, `generate_trailing_data`, and the three
  `*_filter_enabled` keys.

The writable filter fields are flattened, for example
`collision_filter_enabled`, `collision_filter_min_mass`,
`collision_filter_min_speed`, and `collision_filter_min_impulse`; breaking and
trailing use corresponding `min_mass`, `min_speed`, and `min_volume` keys.
`collision_margin_fraction`, `collision_margin_max`, and
`runtime_deterministic` are inspect-only in UE 5.6 and are rejected on write.
The inspector also reads only the six relevant global override CVars under
`settings.runtime.global_cvar_overrides`; this is diagnostic state, global to
the process, and never writable through these routes.

### Cloth component payload

`component_settings` accepts only `enable_simulation`, `suspend_simulation`,
`collide_with_environment`, `cloth_geometry_scale` (`0..10000`),
`teleport_distance_threshold` (non-negative),
`teleport_rotation_threshold` (`0..180` degrees), and `teleport_action`
(`none`, `reset_mode`, `teleport`, or `teleport_and_reset`). The inspector's
`expected_current_fields` map uses the exact stable setting names. Effective
`simulation_enabled`/`simulation_suspended` additionally reflect proxy and
global engine state and are not the optimistic-concurrency fields.

Each `property_updates` entry requires `name` and `value_type`, with optional
`lod_index` (`-1` means all LODs), `expected_current`, and `string_value`.
At most 256 entries are accepted. Accepted value shapes are:

- `float`: `value`;
- `weighted_float`: `low` and `high`;
- `int`: integer `value` in `-16777215..16777215`;
- `bool`: boolean `value`;
- `vector`: three-number `value` array;
- `string`: string `value`.

Per-update `expected_current` may copy any subset of the inspected property
snapshot, including `low_raw_vector`, `high_raw_vector`, `string_value`, or
nested `flags`; numeric comparisons use tolerance. Runtime property updates
are component-local transient overrides. `save_level=true` persists only
serialized component settings, not those overrides or the one-shot
`teleport`/`teleport_and_reset` actions.

Intrinsic runtime properties require `allow_intrinsic_recreate=true` only when
their effective value would change. `recreate_simulation="always"` is an explicit
hard reset and reports `changed=true` even when all supplied values already
match.

Editor Undo for a successful mixed component write covers serialized component
settings only. UE's component re-registration resets transient runtime property
overrides and live simulation state instead of restoring those transient values;
the response exposes `undo_scope`, `runtime_updates_included_in_undo=false`, and
`undo_resets_runtime_overrides_and_simulation` explicitly.

When invoked inside `execute_batch`, all three mutators join the batch's active
outer transaction instead of opening an inner one. Rollback cancels only a
transaction owned by the individual route, so a failed subcommand cannot erase
the Undo record for earlier successful batch steps. Responses expose
`transaction_scope` and `owned_transaction_cancelled`.

## Wall/building cinematic pipeline

The production-oriented surface authors native Unreal assets under
`/Game/_MCP_Temp/ChaosDestructionLearning/Cinematic`. UnrealMCP is only the editor authoring hand:
the resulting Static Mesh, Geometry Collection, Field System actor, Chaos Cache
Collection, Cache Manager, Level Sequence, and Movie Scene tracks must contain
no UnrealMCP class or object reference. Existing source assets are never
overwritten. Output creation defaults to `overwrite_existing=false`.

The current project deliberately uses **Root Mesh Proxy, not Nanite**. This
satisfies the proxy transition path while preserving the user's separate
instruction to omit Nanite from implementation and acceptance.

### Authoring and runtime tools

- `create_geometry_collection_from_mesh(source_static_mesh_paths,
  output_geometry_collection_path, source_transforms=[],
  root_mesh_proxy_static_mesh_path="",
  material_reindex=true, overwrite_existing=false)` converts one or more source
  meshes with `FGeometryCollectionEngineConversion::AppendStaticMesh`. Optional
  location/rotation/scale transforms let one preserved source mesh be assembled
  into walls with openings and multi-part buildings. It
  returns source/transform/geometry/root counts and proxy state while leaving
  every source package unchanged.
- `configure_building_fracture(source_geometry_collection_path,
  output_geometry_collection_path, fracture_method="uniform_voronoi",
  site_count=64, random_seed=1337, cluster_levels=2, cluster_size=8,
  damage_thresholds=[500000,250000,100000], merge_tiny_geometry=true,
  tiny_relative_threshold=.01, collision_object_reduction_percentage=20,
  maximum_collision_particles=60, collision_implicit_type="convex",
  remove_on_sleep=true, remove_on_break=true, sleep_time_seconds=5,
  removal_duration_seconds=1, break_timer_seconds=1, ...)`
  creates a separate fractured
  collection. The output reports rigid count, hierarchy depth, cluster count,
  and per-level counts. Source and output paths must differ.
- `configure_anchor_field(geometry_collection_actor_name, field_actor_name,
  box_center, box_extent, field_magnitude=1, save_level=true)` authors a native
  `AFieldSystemActor` with serialized construction commands and assigns it to
  the Geometry Collection component's `InitializationFields`.
- `trigger_trace_external_strain(operation="begin", actor_name="", start=[],
  end=[], expected_hit=true, ...)` is a bounded
  `begin` / `sample` / `finish` / `abort` observation session. Begin requires
  a valid hit on the requested component and an exact
  `FHitResult.Item` before dispatching real external strain. Expected misses
  dispatch nothing. Hit responses provide the inputs for a later physics-frame
  velocity/state sample without retrying or directly forcing later transforms.
- `record_chaos_cache(operation, cache_manager_actor_name,
  geometry_collection_actor_names, cache_collection_path,
  cache_names=[],
  solver_actor_name="", duration_seconds=2, frame_rate=30,
  timeout_seconds=10, initial_trigger_mode="strain_velocity",
  per_advance_breaks_allowed=8,
  particle_release_throttling_min_count=64,
  particle_release_throttling_max_count=128, ...)` is a bounded
  `begin` / `sample` / `finish` session. PASS requires at least 60 recorded
  frames and at least 2 seconds. World loss, timeout, abort, and finish restore
  the manager/component settings.
- `validate_chaos_cache(operation, cache_manager_actor_name,
  cache_collection_path, sequence_path, geometry_collection_actor_names=[],
  sample_times=[.25,.5,1], scrub_frames=[0,15,30,45,60],
  repeat_count=2, ...)` performs
  repeated forward and reverse-order random access. It returns hashes,
  cross-pass equality, time variation, later-frame physics evidence, and live
  simulation suppression.
- `create_chaos_level_sequence(output_sequence_path, camera_actor_name="", display_rate=30,
  tick_resolution=24000, start_frame=0, end_frame=60, ...)` creates a native
  Level Sequence without replacing an existing asset.
- `bind_chaos_cache_to_sequence(sequence_path, cache_manager_actor_name,
  cache_collection_path,
  section_start_frame=0, section_end_frame=60, cache_play_rate=1,
  cache_start_offset_seconds=0)` adds or reuses the manager possessable and a
  native `UMovieSceneChaosCacheTrack`/section.
- `configure_chaos_sequence_timing(...)` sets the sequence playback range and
  cache section range, rate, start offset, and reverse flag. The response
  explicitly reports the two-second/60-frame gate.
- `inspect_chaos_cinematic_pipeline(geometry_collection_path, sequence_path,
  cache_manager_actor_name, mrq_manifest_path="", ...)` reads hierarchy, Root Mesh Proxy, Anchor,
  Cache, binding, timing, scrub/repeat, MRQ availability/output, and Asset
  Registry dependency evidence. `nanite_used` must be false.

### Required sample matrix

The live suite retains five native sample groups with unique asset/cache/binding
names: a single wall, a wall with a door/window opening, a foundation-anchored
small building, a sequentially collapsing multi-storey building, and multiple
Sequencer shots that reuse the same deterministic cache at different section
offsets. A final manifest records all paths and verifies no collisions between
manager bindings or cache names.

### End-to-end order

1. Convert source meshes to an unfractured Geometry Collection.
2. Create a separate hierarchical fracture output and set the Root Mesh Proxy.
3. Place the collection and configure its foundation Anchor Field.
4. Start cache recording, trigger a real collision or traced external strain,
   poll until at least 60 frames and 2 seconds, then finish.
5. Validate repeated and reverse-order hashes at 0.25, 0.5, and 1.0 seconds and
   verify later-frame live-simulation suppression.
6. Create a 30 fps Level Sequence, bind the Cache Manager/Chaos Cache track,
   set the exact start frame, section offset, and play rate, then verify forward
   and backward scrubbing.
7. Run an isolated low-resolution Movie Render Queue shot, inspect the output,
   and finally load the level, cache, and sequence in a host without UnrealMCP.

Each performance case is executed five times under the same fixed-step,
resolution, and actor-count conditions. The evidence stores median and p95 for
frame, GameThread, Chaos clustering, and active-body counts; a large-building
case additionally enforces an explicit frame-drop and peak-active-body budget.

These tools author and inspect an isolated UE 5.6.1 Chaos Destruction fixture under:

```text
/Game/_MCP_Temp/ChaosDestructionLearning
```

They never start or stop PIE/SIE, switch the user's current map, or persist an UnrealMCP runtime dependency into authored assets. Fixture authoring is blocked while PIE/SIE is active. Runtime operations use an already-running play world only.

## Result semantics

Every successful command keeps three verdicts separate:

- `operation_status`: whether the requested operation executed.
- `assertion_status`: whether the assertions available in that operation passed; `BLOCKED` or `NOT_RUN` means required evidence was unavailable.
- `learning_verdict`: whether the complete learning gate was proven.

An operation or assertion can pass while `learning_verdict=BLOCKED`. No learning area is promoted to PASS until its required five-run A/B test and numeric acceptance condition have been collected.

## `create_chaos_destruction_learning_fixture`

Defaults to `dry_run=true`. With mutation enabled it creates or reuses:

- `SM_ChaosTestBlock`
- `SM_ChaosRootProxy`
- `GC_ChaosBase_96`
- `GC_ChaosTinyOptimized`
- `GC_ChaosRemoval`
- `GC_ChaosRootProxy`
- `CC_ChaosLearning`

The default `configure_current_level=false` is deliberately assets-only. This command never constructs an off-screen `UWorld`. To create the learning level, first use native `safe_new_preview_map` for the exact path `/Game/_MCP_Temp/ChaosDestructionLearning/L_ChaosDestructionLearning`, then call this tool with `configure_current_level=true`. That second call refuses to run unless the current native editor world package matches the exact path, configures the actors in that current world, and saves it through the normal editor map lifecycle.

The base collection has exactly 96 rigid pieces and one root. Twenty-four pieces are deliberately small according to actual world-space convex volume (`RelativeVolume < 0.04`) and are laid out with contact neighbors. Static assertions check hierarchy reciprocity and cycles, Transform-to-Geometry mapping, triangle indices, boundary/non-manifold edges, candidate count, rigid-count reduction after Tiny Geo merging, and convex-volume/world-bounds preservation within one percent.

Only the selected 24 small pieces receive Remove-on-Break metadata. The learning world contains separately configured Sleep-only and Break-only actors, One-Way `-1` and `2` actors with 50 kg witness bodies and a static floor, 32 base instances, 32 Tiny-optimized instances, 32 Root Proxy instances, eight throttling instances, 16 cache instances, a tagged solver, and a tagged cache manager. Root Proxy inspection verifies `UGeometryCollectionRootProxyRenderer`, proxy mesh/transform arrays, Nanite disabled state, and render-state evidence.

Mutation is transactional. Every asset path, `RestCollection`, and runtime actor must remain inside the exact temp root or carry the `MCP_ChaosLearning` tag. The response includes dirty-package snapshots and an Asset Registry dependency audit for forbidden MCP runtime types. The level gate is optional for the assets-only call and mandatory when `configure_current_level=true`.

## `inspect_geometry_collection_performance_guarded`

Read-only inspection reports structural metrics, pre/post-compatible piece metrics, small-piece candidates, Remove-on-Sleep/Break metadata, Root Proxy state, and optional runtime dynamic state. Runtime removal evidence includes active, sleeping, broken, hidden, remaining-visible, and actual disabled PhysicsObject counts.

## `inspect_geometry_collection_runtime_state`

Read one exact Geometry Collection component from an active, begun-play PIE world without mutating or saving anything. `component_name` is required; select the actor with `actor_name` or `actor_label`, and use `include_pieces`/`max_pieces` to bound the response. If more than one begun-play PIE world exists, supply `pie_instance`; otherwise the command blocks instead of sampling an ambiguous world.

The aggregate and per-rigid-piece output separates these signals:

- render-world transform from `GetComponentSpaceTransforms3f()` composed with the component transform;
- PhysicsObject world mass-frame transform, linear velocity, and angular velocity;
- dynamic, sleeping, disabled, and released/broken state;
- Remove-on-Sleep activity and removal `decay`;
- configured per-piece `max_sleep_time_seconds` and `sleep_removal_duration_seconds`, with aggregate min/max ranges;
- visually removed rigid pieces (`decay >= 1`) separately from disabled clustered children.

Do not use the Geometry Collection component transform alone to decide whether a fractured piece moved. Do not treat every disabled PhysicsObject as removed. For removal QA, observe the first sleeping sample, then the first active decay and first `decay >= 1` sample for each eligible rigid piece.

```json
{
  "command": "inspect_geometry_collection_runtime_state",
  "params": {
    "actor_label": "CDV01_ChaosPillar",
    "component_name": "GC_Trunk",
    "pie_instance": 0,
    "include_pieces": true,
    "max_pieces": 512
  }
}
```

## `run_chaos_destruction_performance_smoke`

Supported operations:

- `sample`: read the tagged component's rest/dynamic collection, timer, frame, removal, hidden, broken, and PhysicsObject state. Negative values other than `-1` are decoded as native internal-cluster items and sampled through their mapped child PhysicsObjects.
- `one_way_configure`: configure only interaction level `-1` or `2` for the tagged test actor. The two One-Way collections use the world solver shared by the dynamic witness; other fixtures retain the tagged custom solver.
- `one_way_run`: recursively release one exact rigid leaf, isolate sibling simulation collision, push the leaf's filter/shape state to Chaos, calibrate its PhysicsThread motion, and launch it toward the 50 kg witness. It never applies an impulse or response motion directly to the witness.
- `one_way_sample`: report witness transform/velocity deltas plus PhysicsThread target/witness state, solver identity, shapes, masses, pose error, pair/current/enabled/one-way constraint counts, manifold points, minimum Phi, and solver impulse. PASS requires native same-solver pair-constraint evidence rather than a center-distance proximity heuristic.
- `removal_begin`: start a bounded internal per-frame capture for the tagged Sleep-only or Break-only fixture. Initially disabled clustered leaves remain present until they have first been released and subsequently become hidden or disabled.
- `removal_sample`: read progress without advancing the timeline. `removal_finish` evaluates the completed per-piece first-release, first-sleep, and first-gone timeline against the asset's configured timer ranges within one fixed tick; `removal_abort` removes the ticker without claiming validation.
- `throttle_begin`: restore any stale session, apply solver throttling, capture a baseline, dispatch eight root-cluster destruction requests, and start a bounded internal per-frame ticker. Settings remain active only on the transient PIE solver and are restored automatically on completion, timeout, or world loss.
- `throttle_sample`: read ticker progress without using bridge-call spacing as frame evidence. Samples separate per-component root-cluster transitions from released rigid-particle and active/disabled deltas.
- `throttle_finish`: retrieve a completed timeline, validate the root-cluster per-frame limit and rescheduled releases, and report automatic configuration restoration. `throttle_abort` restores without claiming validation.
- `measure_throttling`: legacy synchronous diagnostic. It restores settings immediately and therefore cannot provide decisive fixed-frame release evidence; prefer the multi-call throttle session.
- `trace_external_strain`: assert expected hit or miss, preserve the exact valid `FHitResult.Item`, dispatch strain/breaking velocity, and return follow-up sample inputs.
- `trace_field_benchmark`: execute the requested traces, require at least 20 valid-item hits, dispatch 100 transient radial external-strain fields, and report both dispatch timings.

Trace follow-up `sample` accepts the item index, expected direction, and dispatch world time. It reads actual dynamic linear velocity after an observed tick and reports the direction dot product.

These calls do not control editor or play lifecycle. Single-call evidence is not a five-run A/B result, so `learning_verdict` remains blocked until the repeated live suite is collected.

## `run_chaos_cache_smoke`

Supported operations:

- `inspect`: read cache count, maximum frames, and maximum duration.
- `configure_record` / `configure_play`: explicitly end evaluation, configure the tagged manager and observed component, then begin evaluation again.
- `record_motion_16`: after `configure_record` and `trigger`, dispatch distinct fracture velocities to the 16 observed components so the recording contains changing state.
- `trigger`: dispatch the existing manager trigger without controlling play lifecycle.
- `finalize_record`: end evaluation/recording, flush writes, and assert nonzero frame/duration gates.
- `playback_validate`: run two random-access playback passes, hash quantized transforms at 0.25, 0.5, and 1.0 seconds, compare both passes, rebuild the observed component physics state as Kinematic, then initialize the cache adapter in Play mode.
- `playback_suppression_sample`: after a later physics frame, verify that every enabled PhysicsObject is kinematic for all 16 instances and report manager mode, BodyInstance simulation flags, and component simulation state.
- `sample`: inspect the recorded frame/duration acceptance gate.

The authored manager defaults to `None`/Triggered so BeginPlay cannot register playback solver callbacks against an empty source collection. `configure_record` duplicates the source Cache Collection into a manager-owned transient PIE object; trigger, record, finalize, and playback then use that duplicate and report whether the source asset was newly dirtied. Triggering the original source collection is rejected. A force rebuild clears stale temp-root caches before saving. Playback validation requires hashes to match between passes and vary across the three sample times, then leaves the adapter active for a later-frame `playback_suppression_sample`. It deliberately reports `performance_ab_available=false`; transform hashing is correctness evidence, not a substitute for a five-run live simulation-versus-cache performance comparison.

## Seven in-scope learning areas and required live evidence

Nanite remains excluded by the user's 2026-08-05 instruction. The later
cinematic request accepts "Nanite or Root Mesh Proxy", so Root Mesh Proxy is
now in scope and Nanite remains `N/A`.

1. Tiny Geo: five A/B runs comparing base and optimized rigid-piece counts and frame cost.
2. Remove on Sleep/Break: five runtime runs showing separate removal timelines and memory/body-count effects.
3. One-Way Interaction: five `-1` versus `2` collision/performance runs with witness evidence.
4. Release throttling: five A/B solver runs with numeric release-per-frame data and verified restoration.
5. Trace + External Strain: expected hit/miss, valid item, post-tick velocity direction, 20-hit gate, and five Trace-versus-Field runs.
6. Chaos Cache: nonzero recording, deterministic two-pass playback hashes, suppression evidence, and five 16-instance live-simulation-versus-cache timing runs.
7. Root Mesh Proxy: native proxy mesh/transform arrays, valid renderer state,
   `nanite_used=false`, and NoMCP asset/sequence loading.

The cinematic completion gate additionally requires the five sample groups,
native Anchor Fields, at least 60 frames and 2 seconds per final cache, native
Chaos Cache tracks, exact section timing, deterministic forward/backward
scrubbing, a successful low-resolution MRQ render, unique multi-cache naming,
and controlled large-building frame/active-body metrics.

Until those live measurements exist, the tools report `learning_verdict=BLOCKED` instead of inferring PASS from authored configuration or dispatch success.

